(Preventing exchange hacks)Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
0 Tc 9.5 0 0 9.5 99 455.7836 Tm
(Bitcoin exchanges have often been targeted by hackers intending )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(to steal BTC. And over the course of the years, vast sums of money )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(have fallen into the wrong hands, most of which can be attributed )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(to a lack of security on these platforms.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(Some of the most notorious exchange hacks in the world of bitcoin )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(date all the way back to the very first time Mt. Gox \(a Tokyo\037based \)Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(bitcoin exchange that has customers all over the world\) became )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(the victim of a hack. One of the website\222s accounts was compro)Tj
(-)Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(mised, ultimately leading to a global price crash from roughly U.S. )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
($32 per bitcoin down to pennies. However, the hackers ran into Mt. )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(Gox\222s daily withdrawal limit of $1,000 at that time, rendering their \)Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(entire operation nearly useless.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(Bitcoinica was a popular bitcoin exchange back in 2012, but that )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(reputation took a major hit when the company lost thousands )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(of bitcoins belonging to customers. Promises were made to pay )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(back customers in full, from Bitcoinica\222s own pocket. However, a )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(second hack followed shortly after, and even more customer funds )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(were lost. In the end, the Bitcoinica story remained unresolved, )Tj
T*
(and there has been no resolution to this very date. The fact that )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(Bitcoinica was linked to Mt. Gox didn\222t help matters either.)Tj
-0.015 Tc 0 -2.316 TD
[(September 2012 spelled the demise of bitcoin exchange BitFloor,)-15( )]TJ
0 -1.158 TD
[(during which 24,000 BTC were stolen by a hacker. To indicate how)-15( )]TJ
0 -1.158 TD
[(lackadaisical exchange security was at that time, the hacker managed)-14.9( )]TJ
0 -1.158 TD
[(to access an unencrypted backup of wallet keys. In the end, most of)-15( )]TJ
0 -1.158 TD
(the lost customer funds were repaid, in U.S. dollars, not in bitcoin.)Tj
0 Tc 0 -2.316 TD
(February 2013 is the darkest period in bitcoin history so far, as this )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(was the time when the second Mt. Gox \223hack\224 occurred, and the )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(exchange shut down for good. Even though the company only held )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(2,000 BTC, users were owed 750,000 BTC in total. The investigation )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(into the missing or stolen BTC is still ongoing at time of writing.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(The list goes on and on. There were bitcoin exchange hacks in )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(2015 as well. There is a long way to go when it comes to creating )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(a proper secure platform where users can trade and store their )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(funds. But until that time comes, you are better off transferring )Tj
T*
(funds out of an exchange at your earliest convenience.)Tj
