(Bitcoin is often touted as a global payment network that includes )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(no transaction fees. Up to a certain extent, that statement is true, )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(but it doesn\222t tell the entire story. No transaction fee is involved \)Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(for the recipient on any bitcoin transaction coming from another )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(user on the network. But sometimes, there is a transaction fee )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(involved, albeit very minimal.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(Transaction fees in the bitcoin world are not included in every )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(transaction. In fact, most bitcoin wallets allow the user to option)Tj
(-)Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(ally include a transaction fee in order to speed up the transaction )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(itself. By speeding up, we mean a transaction including a small )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(fee will be prioritized to be included in the next network block, )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(whereas transactions without fees have a lower priority.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(Certain exceptions to including a transaction fee don\222t affect the )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(transaction speed. In the Bitcoin Core client, if your transaction )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(is smaller than 1,000 bytes in size, has only outputs of 0.01 BTC )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(or higher, and has a high enough priority, a transaction fee is )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(not required. All of these conditions have to be met in order for )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(this exception to be applicable. If these conditions are not met, a )Tj
T*
(standard transaction fee of 0.0001 BTC per thousand bytes will be )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(added. Bitcoin Core client users will notice whenever a transaction )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(fee is included, as the client will prompt the user to either accept )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(or reject the fee associated with the transaction. Rejecting that fee )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(lowers the prioritization and affects the speed at which network )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(confirmations are applied, though.)Tj
