(You might expect that the end-user is in control of bitcoin funds at )Tj
T*
(any given time. However, because the end-user is the only person )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(with the private key belonging to their wallet address\(es\), a more )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(secure solution had to be developed. Whereas leaving one user )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(in control of one wallet for individual usage is okay, things are )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(slightly different for companies, families, or even friends working )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(on a project together. Trust might disintegrate quickly.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(The way a regular bitcoin wallet works is as follows: One user has )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(the private key and has full control over that bitcoin address. If )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(two or more people create a project together, there used to be )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(only one option available to them: )Tj
(T)Tj
(rust one of the users with the )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(private key to the mutual wallet address. If that user wanted to )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(cash out all the funds, the other member\(s\) couldn\222t do anything )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(about it, because they were not in control.)Tj
0 -2.316 TD
(Needless to say, this was a far from perfect solution, which )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(spurred on innovation to give multiple users control of the same )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(wallet. Eventually, a multi\036signature system was developed, where )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(multiple users control the same wallet, yet no single user has )Tj
T*
(full control without the consent of at least one other person in )Tj
0 -1.158 TD
(the group. This is known as a )Tj
/T1_2 1 Tf
(multi\037signature account)Tj
/T1_1 1 Tf
( or )Tj
/T1_2 1 Tf
(multi\037sig )Tj
